<p>Italians began voting on Sunday in a referendum on a constitutional amendment backed by Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni. The measure would separate the careers of judges and public prosecutors as part of a broader judiciary overhaul. The vote comes a year before Italy’s next general election and has turned into a major political test for Meloni and her right-wing coalition. Several outlets say it has become a de facto confidence vote on her government, not just a debate over legal reform. The proposal itself is legally complex, and multiple reports say many voters do not fully understand it, which could shape both turnout and the final result. Supporters say the changes would make the justice system more balanced. Critics warn they could weaken judicial independence and cast the campaign as an attack on judges.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/XfaZx3X8ryDb5k1fDlhV3xvpv4WM67CNjop9i2eNhytU1nrlBjmydcpMrwV-cxn-Y48lun9fq7SGH_u0ZYU1y6glT6VLQRVIdpWuwpEAjvDAZXa8DpthnI7vGY0iAoZ9daoXyQmfcgHscTgxdVWlMZRPwzutVm4KO190Alh_yAts5jzEFq5DeV48iZVJnAVqJDek6yVHGp5pyPxNMnf7uydqN28' alt='Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni of Italy waving to supporters at a campaign event this month.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Neck-and-neck polls: Surveys cited before voting began showed an extremely close race, adding to the uncertainty around a referendum already seen as politically important for Meloni’s coalition.</li><li>Campaign tone: The campaign grew heated, including a Brothers of Italy flyer that Facebook later removed after it targeted judges and feminists while urging a “yes” vote.</li><li>Opposition divide: Reuters said the referendum is also a test for a divided opposition, which has struggled to offer voters a single, unified message during the campaign.</li><li>Obscure subject: BBC described the referendum’s subject as dry and obscure — an unusual setting for a vote with national political weight.</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/euro